[Event "London System: the pyramid"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/6QUAuVXa"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Time to learn the London System: pyramid up, then attack. Open with the queen pawn. [%cal Yd2d4] }
1. d4 { The queen pawn. Solid, but there is a real attack hiding behind it. } (1. e4 { The other great first move, and just as good. } 1... e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 { A whole different world of chess. The London needs the queen pawn. }) (1. Nf3 { Flexible and strong, and it often comes to the same thing. } 1... d5 2. d4 Nf6 { You got there anyway. We simply play the pawn first. }) (1. c4 { The English. Excellent, and nothing like what we are learning. } 1... e5 2. g3 Nf6 { A fine opening for another day. }) 1... d5 { He copies you. Now the move that names the opening: the dark bishop out to f4. [%cal Yc1f4] } 2. Bf4 { There it is. The bishop gets outside the pawn chain before you shut it in. [%csl Gf4] } (2. c4 { The queens gambit, one of the most respected replies in all of chess. } 2... e6 3. Nf3 dxc4 { Wonderful chess, but not what we came for today. }) (2. Nf3 { Also excellent. But the bishop must come out before the e pawn. } 2... Nf6 3. c4 dxc4 { A different game. In the London the bishop goes first, every time. }) (2. e3 { And there it is: your bishop is now stuck behind its own pawn. } 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 e6 { The bad bishop is exactly what the London avoids. [%csl Rc1] }) (2. Nc3 { The knight blocks the square your c pawn wants. } 2... c5 3. Bf4 Nc6 { You played the bishop anyway, and the c pawn is stuck at home. }) 2... c5 { He hits your centre. Never take that pawn. Build instead: the e pawn, one square. [%cal Ye2e3] } 3. e3 { Brick one. It also opens a door home for the bishop. [%csl Ge3] } (3. dxc5 { Grabbing the pawn. He wins it straight back with time to spare. } 3... e6 4. e3 Bxc5 { His bishop lands on a lovely square for free. Never take on c5. }) (3. c3 { The other brick, but out of order. His queen jumps out at once. } 3... Qb6 4. Nf3 Nc6 { She is at your b pawn already. The e pawn first stops all that. [%cal Rb6b2] }) (3. Nf3 { Developing before building. He breaks up your centre. } 3... cxd4 4. c3 dxc3 { You are a pawn down and hoping. Build first. }) (3. Bxb8 { Taking a knight with your best piece. Never trade this bishop away. } 3... Rxb8 4. e3 c4 { You gave up the London bishop for a sleeping knight. [%csl Rf4] }) (3. e4 { Striking in the middle instead of building the pyramid. } 3... dxe4 4. d5 Nf6 { He grabs the pawn and now you are the worse one. Build instead. }) 3... Nf6 { He develops. Answer his c pawn with yours, one square, and the pyramid is up. [%cal Yc2c3] } 4. c3 { Brick two. Meet his c pawn with yours, and never ever capture on c5. [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge3][%cal Rd4c5] } (4. Nf3 { Also good, and just as natural for the pieces. } 4... e6 5. c3 Bd6 { It transposes. Building the pyramid first is simply clearer. }) (4. Nd2 { The knight before the brick. Playable, but he pokes at b2. } 4... Qb6 5. Ngf3 e6 { His queen is out and your b pawn needs watching. }) (4. dxc5 { The capture again. It is still a gift. } 4... e6 5. Nf3 Bxc5 { He develops with tempo and the tension has gone. }) (4. Nc3 { The knight takes the square your c pawn needs. } 4... cxd4 5. exd4 Bg4 { Your pyramid can never be built now. }) 4... e6 { He shuts his own bishop in. Now the queens knight, and it goes to d2, not c3. [%cal Yb1d2] } 5. Nd2 { Queens knight first. Keeping f3 free for the other one really matters. [%csl Gd2] } (5. Nf3 { Just as good by the numbers, and it costs you the f3 square. } 5... Bd6 6. Bb5+ Nc6 { Fine, but now your other knight has nowhere natural to go. }) (5. Bd3 { The bishop before the knights. He hits back at once. } 5... Qb6 6. Qc2 Nh5 { His knight pokes at your best piece before you are set. [%cal Rh5f4] }) (5. Be2 { Modest, and it does nothing for the plan. } 5... Bd6 6. Nf3 O-O { Level. The knight to d2 keeps the whole idea alive. }) (5. Bg3 { Retreating before anyone has asked. } 5... Ne4 6. Nd2 Nxg3 { He trades off your proud bishop for nothing. [%csl Rg3] }) 5... Nc6 { Now the king knight to f3, where the other one would have been in its way. [%cal Yg1f3] } 6. Ngf3 { Now the king knight. Every piece is heading for the middle. [%csl Gf3] } (6. Bd3 { The bishop first, and he releases the tension for you. } 6... Bd6 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { He is comfortable and your knight is still at home. }) (6. Bg3 { Stepping back before the question has been asked. } 6... Be7 7. Ngf3 Nh5 { He pokes at the bishop anyway and you lost a move. }) (6. Be2 { Quiet, but the knight was the piece that wanted the square. } 6... Bd6 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { Level, and your plan has slowed right down. }) (6. h3 { A little air for the king, at the wrong moment. } 6... Bd6 7. Bxd6 Qxd6 { He offers the trade and you had no good way to decline. }) 6... Bd6 { The standoff. Do not trade: step the bishop back to g3 and keep the tension. [%cal Yf4g3] } 7. Bg3 { You step back and keep the tension. The pyramid stands: bishop g3, pawns c3, d4 and e3, both knights out and ready. [%cal Gf4g3] } (7. Bxd6 { Never trade here. That bishop is the best piece you own. } 7... Qxd6 8. Bb5 O-O { His queen lands beautifully and the e pawn is coming. }) (7. Ne5 { Too early. He kicks the knight and you have no pawn to hold it. } 7... Qc7 8. Nxc6 Bxf4 { You had to trade off and he is perfectly fine. }) (7. dxc5 { You gave away the pyramid, and the pyramid is the whole plan. } 7... Bxc5 8. Bd3 O-O { Playable, but everything you built has gone. }) (7. Bb5 { A pin on the far side while your bishop is being attacked. } 7... Bxf4 8. exf4 Qb6 { He trades off your best piece and your pawns are ugly. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: never let him play e5"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/VtRh8soC"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 5 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R_b_KQkq_-_5_7"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid is up: bishop g3, pawns c3, d4 and e3. Now watch what he does with his king. }
7... O-O { He is safe. Finish your own development, and point at his king while you do. } 8. Bd3 { The last piece out, and it stares down the diagonal at h7. [%cal Gd3h7] } (8. Bb5 { A pin on the other wing. Playable, but it aims at nothing. } 8... Be7 9. Qe2 c4 { His pawn grabs space and your bishop is on the wrong side. }) (8. Be2 { Modest and safe, and it wastes the best diagonal you own. } 8... b6 9. O-O Bb7 { Level. That bishop wanted the road to h7. }) (8. Ne5 { The knight jumps a move too early, before it can be propped up. } 8... Bxe5 9. dxe5 Nd7 { He trades it off and your centre pawn is loose. }) (8. Qc2 { The queen takes the square your bishop wants. } 8... b6 9. Bd3 Qc7 { You had to move again and he got a free developing move. }) 8... Re8 { The rook backs a plan. That pawn wants to advance and free his whole game. [%csl Re6] } 9. Ne5 { Sit on the square first. Nothing in this opening matters more. [%csl Ge5] } (9. O-O { Castling looks obvious. Watch what he plays next. } 9... e5 10. dxe5 Nxe5 { And there it goes. His game opens and yours went flat. [%csl Re5] }) (9. Bxd6 { You traded, he is comfortable, and the e pawn is still coming. } 9... Qxd6 10. Bb5 Bd7 { His queen is happy and nothing stops the push. }) (9. dxc5 { The tension goes and the pawn arrives anyway. } 9... Bxc5 10. O-O e5 { Nothing gained, and he has the freer game. }) (9. Bb5 { A pin instead of the outpost. He never even notices. } 9... Bxg3 10. hxg3 Qb6 { Your best piece is gone and e5 is still his. }) 9... Qc7 { He piles up on your knight. Do not move it. Feed it. } 10. f4 { The stonewall. Pawns on c3, d4, e3 and f4, and now you go hunting. [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge3,Gf4] } (10. Nxc6 { Trading off the proud knight. Also good, and much quieter. } 10... Qxc6 11. Nf3 b6 { Perfectly pleasant. But the pawn wall is the London dream. }) (10. Ndf3 { Propping with the knight instead of the pawn. Also playable. } 10... Bd7 11. a4 h6 { Solid. The f pawn is simply more menacing. }) (10. O-O { Castling now, and his knight takes your outpost for free. } 10... Nxe5 11. dxe5 Bxe5 { He wins the pawn and your whole plan with it. [%cal Rc6e5] }) (10. Bb5 { Pinning when you should be propping. } 10... Bd7 11. Nxd7 Nxd7 { You helped him untangle and the outpost has gone. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he grabs space with c4"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/OZufLPbi"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 5 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R_b_KQkq_-_5_7"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid is up again, and this time he looks to the queen side. Space can be a trap. }
7... c4 { He locks the queen side. But that pawn can never capture anything again. [%csl Rc4] } 8. b3 { Ask the question at once. A chain is only as strong as its base. [%cal Gb3c4] } (8. Be2 { Quiet development. Also good, but you give him a free move. } 8... O-O 9. O-O b5 { He props the pawn up first. The same idea, one move slower. }) (8. Qc2 { The queen is useful there, and the b pawn is coming anyway. } 8... b5 9. Be2 O-O { Also fine. Hitting the base straight away is sharper. }) (8. a4 { The other wing pawn. Just as good, and a shade less direct. } 8... O-O 9. Be2 b6 { Equally pleasant. Both pawns end up joining in. }) (8. Bxd6 { Trading before you have to. Do it when it helps you. } 8... Qxd6 9. Be2 b5 { His queen is fine and the chain is untouched. }) 8... b5 { He props it up. Now two of his pawns are stuck on light squares. } 9. Bxd6 { Now the trade is right: that was his best piece, and his pawns are fixed. [%cal Gg3d6] } (9. a4 { The other pawn first. Exactly as good, just the other order. } 9... b4 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 { The very same position. Either order works. }) (9. Be2 { Quiet, and it lets him finish the job on the queen side. } 9... O-O 10. O-O Bb7 { Level. You had a trade that improved your position. }) (9. Ne5 { The outpost, but here his pawns just take it away. } 9... Ne7 10. Qc2 a6 { The knight has no support and the plan stalls. }) (9. bxc4 { Releasing it yourself. Now his pawn on c4 is comfortable. } 9... bxc4 10. e4 Nxe4 { You opened lines for him and lost a pawn doing it. }) 9... Qxd6 { He takes back. Bring the second pawn into the attack. } 10. a4 { Two pawns aim at his one on b5, and the pressure on that chain is real right now. [%cal Ga4b5] } (10. Qc2 { Solid, but slow. His chain gets time to settle. } 10... O-O 11. a4 cxb3 { You played it anyway, a move behind. }) (10. Be2 { Developing when the pawns were doing the work. } 10... O-O 11. a4 b4 { Fine, but you gave him the free move. }) (10. Qc1 { The queen shuffles and the queen side waits. } 10... Rb8 11. a4 a6 { He gets organised first. Much less than you had. }) (10. a3 { The little push does not attack anything at all. } 10... e5 11. dxe5 Nxe5 { He breaks in the middle while you fiddle on the wing. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he trades on g3"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/8eMnToq5"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R b KQkq - 5 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pp3ppp/2nbpn2/2pp4/3P4/2P1PNB1/PP1N1PPP/R2QKB1R_b_KQkq_-_5_7"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid is up, bishop on g3 and all. If he trades it off, recapture the right way and you are the happy one. }
7... Bxg3 { There it goes. Two pawns can take back. Which one, and why? } 8. hxg3 { The h pawn, towards the centre, and it opens a file straight at his king. [%cal Gh1h8] } (8. fxg3 { The wrong pawn. Your king is draughty and the rook sees nothing. } 8... Ng4 9. Qe2 Qc7 { His knight jumps in through the hole you just made. [%csl Rg4] }) (8. dxc5 { You forgot to take back. Count the pieces. } 8... Bc7 9. b4 O-O { Your bishop is simply gone. You are a piece down. [%csl Rg3] }) 8... Qb6 { He goes after b2 while you are busy. Guard it and keep building. } 9. Qc2 { She covers b2 and already eyes the long diagonal. Everything holds. [%cal Gc2b2] } (9. Qb3 { Facing her down. Just as good, and it offers a trade. } 9... O-O 10. Be2 Ng4 { Equal value. Keeping the queens on is a little more fun. }) (9. Rb1 { The rook defends. It works, it is just very passive. } 9... h6 10. Bd3 Ng4 { Solid but sleepy. The queen does the job better. }) (9. b3 { You hold the pawn but put another one on a light square. } 9... h6 10. Ne5 cxd4 { Playable, and your queen side is full of holes. }) (9. dxc5 { Grabbing a pawn and leaving b2 to its fate. } 9... Qxb2 10. c4 O-O { He takes and your queen side falls apart. [%cal Rb6b2] }) (9. Qc1 { The queen goes to the corner and does nothing at all. } 9... cxd4 10. exd4 Ng4 { He breaks first and you are still untangling. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: the queen comes to b6"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/BC1IZO1G"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pp2pppp/5n2/2pp4/3P1B2/2P1P3/PP3PPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 0 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pp2pppp/5n2/2pp4/3P1B2/2P1P3/PP3PPP/RN1QKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_4"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid is already built. If he brings the knight out early, watch his queen next. }
4... Nc6 { He develops. Play the knight where it also guards the b pawn. } 5. Nd2 { Queens knight to d2. In a moment you will see exactly why. [%csl Gd2] } (5. Nf3 { The other knight, and it leaves b2 with nobody watching it. } 5... Nh5 6. dxc5 Nxf4 7. exf4 e6 { He trades your best piece off. Playable, but we want d2. }) (5. a4 { A wing move at the moment your pieces should come out. } 5... e6 6. Nf3 Nh5 { He pokes at the bishop and you have gained nothing. }) (5. Be2 { Quiet, and it leaves the queen side unguarded. } 5... Qb6 6. Qc1 cxd4 { Your queen is driven to the corner to defend. }) (5. Bd3 { The bishop is fine there, but b2 is loose behind it. } 5... Qb6 6. b3 Bg4 { You had to weaken the queen side to hold it. }) 5... Qb6 { There she is, eyeing b2. Now you see why the knight went to d2. } 6. Qb3 { Queen faces queen. Neither can take, so nothing happens at all. [%cal Gb3b6] } (6. Qc2 { Also a defence, but she does not look him in the eye. } 6... Nh5 7. Be5 cxd4 { He trades off the bishop and you are the busier one. }) (6. Ngf3 { Developing and leaving the b pawn to its fate. } 6... Qxb2 7. Rb1 Qxc3 { She helps herself to two pawns. You have some activity, not enough. [%cal Rb6b2] }) (6. Bd3 { The bishop comes out and the queen walks in. } 6... Qxb2 7. Ne2 cxd4 { A free pawn and your position is loose. [%cal Rb6b2] }) (6. Qc1 { Hiding in the corner. It holds, and it does nothing else. } 6... cxd4 7. exd4 e5 { He breaks in the middle while your queen sulks. }) 6... c4 { He gains space with tempo. But that pawn has stopped attacking anything. [%csl Rc4] } 7. Qc2 { Step aside and keep the queens on. A trade would help only him. [%csl Gc2] } (7. Qxb6 { Never trade here. Look what it does to his rook and pawns. } 7... axb6 8. a3 b5 { His rook wakes up on the open file and his pawns get strong. }) (7. Nxc4 { The pawn is poisoned. Count the defenders first. } 7... dxc4 8. Bxc4 e6 { His pawn takes yours and you gave up a whole piece. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (7. Bxc4 { The other capture, and it loses a piece in exactly the same way. } 7... dxc4 8. Nxc4 Qa6 { A piece for a pawn. You are lost. [%cal Rd5c4] }) (7. Qd1 { Going backwards hands over the b pawn for nothing. } 7... Qxb2 8. Ne2 Bf5 { She eats and stays safe. You are worse. }) 7... g6 { He heads for the fianchetto. Meanwhile your break is sitting there ready. } 8. e4 { There it is. His c pawn went past you, so the middle is yours. [%cal Ge4d5] } (8. h3 { Solid and slow. But the break was the whole reason you let him push. } 8... Bg7 9. Ngf3 O-O { Level. You had the middle on offer and left it. }) (8. Be2 { Development when a pawn break was screaming at you. } 8... Bg7 9. h3 Bf5 { Equal. His pieces come out and the moment is gone. }) (8. Bg3 { Tucking the bishop away for no reason at all. } 8... Bf5 9. Qc1 Nh5 { He chases it anyway and you are slightly worse. }) (8. Ngf3 { Natural, and it lets him poke at the bishop first. } 8... Nh5 9. Bg3 Bf5 { The break is gone and he is comfortable. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he goes for the fianchetto"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/mhcNK56U"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_1"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The queen pawn is out, and he can answer many ways. Keep going: the bishop still comes out. }
1... Nf6 { The knight first this time. Your plan does not change. } 2. Bf4 { The London bishop, outside the pawn chain, exactly as before. [%csl Gf4] } (2. c4 { The queens gambit, a beautiful opening in its own right. } 2... e6 3. g3 d5 { Marvellous chess. But we are learning the London today. }) (2. Nf3 { Also excellent, and also a different opening. } 2... d5 3. c4 e6 { Fine, but the bishop is what makes this the London. }) (2. e3 { And your bishop is shut in behind its own pawn again. } 2... d5 3. Nf3 e6 { The bad bishop is exactly what we are avoiding. [%csl Rc1] }) (2. Bg5 { The other bishop square. Playable, but he chases it. } 2... d5 3. e3 c5 { It gets traded for a knight and the London idea has gone. }) 2... g6 { The bishop is coming to g7. Against this, forget the pyramid. } 3. Nc3 { Knight to c3, not the c pawn. Now the e pawn is free to jump. [%csl Gc3] } (3. e3 { Quiet and solid, but passive against the fianchetto. } 3... Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O { Playable. Against g6 you want the bigger centre. }) (3. c4 { A fine opening, and a very popular one. } 3... c5 4. d5 b5 { Good chess, but it is no longer the London. }) (3. Nd2 { The other knight square. Also good, and a shade more passive. } 3... c5 4. e3 Bg7 { Equal. On c3 the knight backs the e pawn push. }) (3. Nf3 { Natural development, but it does not help the e pawn. } 3... Bg7 4. Nc3 d5 { You reached the same place, one move behind. }) 3... d5 { He stops your e pawn dead. So the attack comes down the other side. } 4. e3 { Quietly solid, and now the rook pawn is free to run. [%csl Ge3] } (4. Nf3 { Also good, and just as natural here. } 4... Bg7 5. e3 O-O { The same position, the other order. Both are fine. }) (4. Nb5 { The knight raid achieves very little here. } 4... Na6 5. Nc3 Nh5 { He guards c7 and you have to come home again. }) (4. h4 { The storm, before you have a square for your king. } 4... Bg7 5. e3 h5 { He stops the pawn and you have a hole on g4. }) (4. h3 { A quiet move that does not prepare anything. } 4... Bg7 5. Nb5 Na6 { Level. The e pawn was the move that mattered. }) 4... Bg7 { The bishop takes the long diagonal. Now start the storm. } 5. h4 { The rook pawn charges. Bold rather than best, but very hard to meet. [%cal Gh4h5] } (5. Nf3 { Calm development, and perfectly respectable. } 5... O-O 6. Be2 c5 { A small edge and no fireworks. We came for the storm. }) (5. Be2 { Solid and quiet, and just as good by the numbers. } 5... O-O 6. Nf3 c5 { Equal. The rook pawn asks a much harder question. }) (5. Nb5 { The raid again, and again he simply guards. } 5... Na6 6. Nc3 Nb8 { He shuffles back and so do you. Nothing gained. }) (5. Bd3 { The bishop on the wrong diagonal against a fianchetto. } 5... O-O 6. h3 c5 { His bishop on g7 is the better one now. }) 5... O-O { He castles into the storm. Now keep it coming. } 6. h5 { The pawn arrives. He really should leave it alone. [%csl Gh5] } (6. Nb5 { The knight raid instead of the pawn. Also playable. } 6... Na6 7. Nc3 c5 { Equal. You had a storm going; finish it. }) (6. Nf3 { Developing and letting the attack go cold. } 6... c5 7. Ne5 Qa5 { He hits back in the middle and your pawn on h4 is loose. }) (6. Be2 { Quiet, and now he stops the pawn himself. } 6... h5 7. Nf3 c5 { The whole idea has gone. Level. }) (6. Qd2 { The queen comes out before the pawn has done its job. } 6... c5 7. O-O-O Qa5 { He counters on the wing where your king is going. }) 6... Nxh5 { He grabbed it, and that was the mistake — the pawn push to c5 kept everything solid instead. Now make him pay for it. } 7. Rxh5 { The rook goes in. Give the exchange and rip his king wide open. [%cal Gh5h7] } (7. Be5 { You backed off and the whole point of the rook pawn has gone. } 7... Nf6 8. f3 Nbd7 { He is a pawn up and perfectly safe. You are worse. }) (7. Bg5 { A pin that does nothing while he is a pawn up. } 7... c5 8. Be2 Nf6 { The knight walks home and you have nothing at all. }) (7. Qf3 { Aiming at the knight, but he simply trades. } 7... Nxf4 8. Qxf4 c6 { Your best piece is gone and so is the pawn. }) (7. Bh2 { Tucking the bishop away with a pawn missing. } 7... c5 8. g4 cxd4 { He breaks in the middle and you are worse. }) 7... gxh5 { He has to take. Now bring the queen. } 8. Qxh5 { You have given the exchange for this: his king with no pawns left around it, and every piece you own still pointing at it. [%csl Rg8] } (8. Qf3 { The wrong square. He develops with tempo and you are in trouble. } 8... Bg4 9. Qxd5 Nc6 { His bishop comes with gain of time and your queen is chased. }) (8. Bd3 { You never took the pawn back and his king gets safe again. } 8... Bg4 9. f3 Bc8 { A whole exchange for nothing. You are lost. }) (8. Nf3 { Developing when the king was one move from falling. } 8... Bg4 9. Qd2 f6 { The attack is over and you are the exchange down. }) (8. Be5 { The bishop instead of the queen. Far too slow. } 8... Bg4 9. Be2 f6 { He kicks everything away and you have nothing. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: the copycat bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/EIjmWt1X"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/3P1B2/8/PPP1PPPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 1 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/3P1B2/8/PPP1PPPP/RN1QKBNR_b_KQkq_-_1_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid's bishop is already out on f4. If he copies it, the bill arrives at b7. }
2... Bf5 { He mirrors you. But his bishop has just left the b pawn on its own. [%csl Rb7] } 3. c4 { Hit the middle while his queen side is loose. This is the sharp way. [%cal Gc4d5] } (3. e3 { The safe way, and Carlsen has played it. } 3... e6 4. c4 Nf6 { Solid and pleasant. But there is nothing to bite on. }) (3. Nf3 { The same idea a move later. Also perfectly good. } 3... e6 4. c4 Nf6 { You got there anyway. The pawn can simply go first. }) (3. Nd2 { Quiet development while his queen side sits loose. } 3... e6 4. e3 Nf6 { Level. You turned down the sharp line. }) (3. Nc3 { The knight takes the square the c pawn wanted. } 3... e6 4. e3 Nf6 { Solid, but your best plan has gone. }) 3... e6 { He shores up the middle. Develop and pile on. } 4. Nc3 { Every piece you add points straight at that queen side. [%csl Gc3] } (4. cxd5 { Releasing the tension and letting him get solid. } 4... exd5 5. Nc3 Nf6 { Playable, but the pressure you had is gone. }) (4. Nf3 { Also good, and a shade quieter. } 4... dxc4 5. Nbd2 Nf6 { Fine. The queen knight is the one that belongs on c3. }) (4. e3 { Quiet. The b pawn is safe again and the whole idea has gone. } 4... Nf6 5. Nd2 c5 { Level, and he is comfortable. }) (4. Qa4+ { A check that only makes him improve. } 4... Nd7 5. Nc3 Nf6 { He blocks and develops. You gained nothing. }) 4... Nf6 { He develops. Now the move the whole line was built for. } 5. Qb3 { The queen aims at b7 and d5 at once. He has no comfortable answer. [%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] } (5. Nf3 { Sensible and good, but it lets him consolidate. } 5... Nbd7 6. c5 c6 { Pleasant. The queen sortie is the point of this whole line. }) (5. e3 { You let him off. The b pawn is covered and he is fine. } 5... Bb4 6. Nf3 O-O { Level, and the copycat has got away with it. }) (5. Rc1 { The rook lines up on a file that is not open yet. } 5... dxc4 6. e3 Nd5 { He grabs the pawn and your rook does nothing. }) (5. c5 { Locking the middle and letting him breathe. } 5... Be7 6. e3 O-O { The tension is gone and so is your edge. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he throws the b pawn at you"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/tPTikqlk"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pppppppp/5n2/8/3P1B2/8/PPP1PPPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 2 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
