[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid's bishop is out. If he strikes at your centre with the c pawn, do not take it. }
2... c5 { He hits d4. Taking hands him the centre. What does the pawn want to do? } 3. d5 { Push past. Now his pawn on c5 bites on nothing at all. [%csl Gd5] } (3. e3 { Playable. But once he plays c5, that pawn wants to go forward. } 3... d6 4. Nc3 Qb6 { His queen pokes at you. Playable, but pushing the pawn was stronger. }) (3. dxc5 { He wins the pawn straight back and you have nothing to show. } 3... Na6 4. f3 Nxc5 { The knight comes to a great square with tempo. }) (3. c3 { Propping a pawn that wanted to run instead. } 3... Qb6 4. Nd2 cxd4 { He trades and hits b2. Your setup is tangled. }) (3. Nc3 { Developing while the centre is still up in the air. } 3... cxd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight gains time on your queen. }) 3... b5 { The wing thrust. Do not chase it. Answer in the centre, where it counts. } 4. e4 { Straight down the middle. His pawn on b5 is miles from the action. [%csl Ge4] } (4. Nf3 { Solid development, and also good. } 4... Bb7 5. c4 d6 { Perfectly fine. But the pawn to e4 is simply stronger. }) (4. f3 { Propping e4 before you play it. Also playable. } 4... g6 5. e4 Bg7 { You got there anyway, a move slower. }) (4. c4 { Grabbing is playable, but he gets the open lines he paid for. } 4... b4 5. Nf3 g6 { His pawn cramps you and the queen side opens for him. }) (4. e3 { The little push when the big one was available. } 4... Qa5+ 5. c3 Bb7 { A check, and your centre is modest. }) 4... Nxe4 { He grabs the pawn. Now hit the knight and look behind it. } 5. Qf3 { She attacks the knight and stares down the long diagonal. [%cal Gf3e4,Gf3a8] } (5. Bd3 { Also good, but the bishop only does one job. } 5... Qa5+ 6. Nd2 Nf6 { Fine. The queen does two jobs at once. }) (5. Nf3 { Developing and leaving him a clean extra pawn. } 5... Bb7 6. Nc3 Nxc3 { He keeps the extra pawn and the game is only level. The queen was sharper. }) (5. c3 { Too slow. He keeps the pawn and gets his pieces out. } 5... a6 6. a4 Nf6 { He keeps the pawn and untangles. Level, and you had far better. }) (5. Be2 { Quiet development with a pawn missing. } 5... g6 6. Bf3 Nf6 { You spent two moves on the bishop and he is fine. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: the b pawn is bait"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/x0SiPPyM"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pp1ppppp/5n2/2pP4/5B2/8/PPP1PPPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pp1ppppp/5n2/2pP4/5B2/8/PPP1PPPP/RN1QKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The pyramid pawn is on d5. If his queen comes hunting, let her have the pawn. }
3... Qb6 { She hits b2 and d5 at once. Do not defend. What is worth more? [%csl Rb2] } 4. Nc3 { Ignore the pawn. Every piece you get out is worth more than it is. [%csl Gc3] } (4. Bc1 { Crawling backwards to save a pawn. He is delighted. } 4... c4 5. Nc3 e6 { You lost three moves with that bishop. }) (4. b3 { Guarding with the pawn invites her deeper into your camp. } 4... Qb4+ 5. Bd2 Qd4 { She sits in the middle of your position. }) (4. Nf3 { Also developing, but it leaves c3 unguarded behind you. } 4... Qxb2 5. Be5 Qb4+ { She grabs and checks. Your knight belongs on c3. }) (4. e4 { Building the centre and leaving the queen a free lunch. } 4... Qxb2 5. Nd2 Nxe4 { She eats and his knight takes another one. }) 4... Qxb2 { She takes. Now there is only one move that holds everything. } 5. Bd2 { The bishop covers c3 and asks her to go home. [%cal Gd2c3] } (5. Be5 { Look again: that knight was guarding d5, and now it is not. } 5... Nxd5 6. Qb1 Qxb1+ { You lose the pawn and the queens come off. }) (5. e4 { The knight on c3 has nothing behind it. She simply takes. } 5... Qxc3+ 6. Bd2 Qe5 { A whole knight for nothing. [%cal Rb2c3] }) (5. f3 { The same mistake with a different pawn. Cover c3 first. } 5... Qxc3+ 6. Bd2 Qe5 { The knight is gone. Always the bishop to d2. }) (5. Nb5 { Moving the knight away instead of guarding it. } 5... Qxb5 6. f3 Qa5+ { You gave up a whole piece. [%cal Rb2b5] }) 5... Qb6 { She retreats. He spent three moves on one pawn; you spend them on the centre. } 6. e4 { Now build. Two big pawns and a huge lead in development. [%csl Ge4,Gd5] } (6. f4 { The other big pawn. Just as good, and the same idea. } 6... Qd8 7. e4 d6 { Equal value. Both pawns are coming anyway. }) (6. Rb1 { Chasing her with the rook instead of taking the centre. } 6... Qd8 7. f4 a6 { Also playable. But the middle is worth more than the chase. }) (6. a4 { A wing move when the centre was the whole point. } 6... g6 7. e4 d6 { You built it anyway, a move behind. }) (6. Nf3 { Developing when the pawns should go first. } 6... g6 7. e4 d6 { Level-ish. The pawn wanted that square. }) 6... d6 { He props the middle. Add the second pawn. } 7. f4 { Two big pawns and every piece ready. This is what the pawn bought. [%csl Ge4,Gf4] } (7. a4 { Also good, taking squares from his queen side. } 7... g6 8. f4 Bg7 { Fine. The f pawn simply comes with more menace. }) (7. Bc4 { The bishop is fine there, but the pawn is faster. } 7... g6 8. f4 Bg7 { Playable. You played f4 anyway. }) (7. Rb1 { The rook again, and again the pawns were the point. } 7... Qd8 8. f4 a6 { Slower, and he gets organised. }) (7. h4 { A wing storm with the centre unfinished. } 7... g6 8. h5 Nxh5 { He simply takes and you have nothing for it. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he clamps with d6 and e5"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/ablGuCod"]
[FEN "rnbqkb1r/pp1ppppp/5n2/2pP4/5B2/8/PPP1PPPP/RN1QKBNR b KQkq - 0 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkb1r/pp1ppppp/5n2/2pP4/5B2/8/PPP1PPPP/RN1QKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ This is the hardest set up for the pyramid to meet. One bishop move solves all your problems. }
3... d6 { He is going to build a wall with e5 and squeeze your bishop. Where does the knight go? } 4. Nc3 { The knight first. The c pawn stays home, so that square is free. [%csl Gc3] } (4. Nf3 { Natural, but it does nothing about the coming wall. } 4... e5 5. dxe6 Bxe6 { You had to release the centre and he develops for free. }) (4. c4 { The pawn takes the square your knight wanted. } 4... e5 5. Bg5 Be7 { Playable, but your queen knight has no home now. }) (4. a4 { A wing move when a wall is being built at you. } 4... e6 5. c4 e5 { The a pawn does nothing here. Get a piece out. }) (4. h4 { A storm before you have solved the bishop problem. } 4... e5 5. Bg5 Qb6 { He hits b2 and your bishop is still short of squares. }) 4... e5 { There is the wall, and your bishop is suddenly very short of squares. [%csl Rf4][%cal Re5f4] } 5. Bg5 { Not backwards, sideways: the pin means that knight cannot help defend, and your bishop was never at risk from any trap. [%cal Gg5f6] } (5. dxe6 { Taking in passing. Playable, but you give up the whole clamp. } 5... Bxe6 6. Nf3 Nc6 { His pieces come out with tempo and the space is gone. }) (5. Bg3 { You retreated, and now the bishop is buried behind his pawns. } 5... Qb6 6. Rb1 Qa5 { Three moves for that bishop and it does nothing. [%csl Rg3] }) (5. Bxe5 { Count the defenders: his d pawn is guarding that square. } 5... dxe5 6. e4 c4 { A whole piece for one pawn. You are lost. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (5. Bc1 { All the way home. Three moves for that bishop and nothing to show. } 5... e4 6. g3 Qe7 { His pawns roll forward and you are cramped. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he plays c5 on move one"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/f0JBtQXT"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_1"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ No knight, no bishop, just a pawn — no pyramid today. This one needs a different set up entirely. }
1... c5 { He hits your pawn before developing anything. Punish that. } 2. d5 { Push. He gave you the whole centre before moving a single piece. [%csl Gd5] } (2. e3 { Now it is a dull symmetrical game and you gave your chance away. } 2... d5 3. c4 e6 { Level. The push was there for the taking. }) (2. e4 { The big centre, but he simply trades your pawn off. } 2... cxd4 3. Nf3 e5 { He is fine and your extra pawn in the middle has gone. }) (2. dxc5 { You can try to hold it. But pushing gives you space for free. } 2... e6 3. e4 Bxc5 { He wins it back and develops with tempo. }) (2. c3 { Propping the pawn when it wanted to run past. } 2... Nf6 3. d5 e6 { You pushed anyway, one move later. }) 2... Nf6 { At last a piece. Bring out the knight, and keep the c pawn home. } 3. Nc3 { That square is for a piece, not a pawn. [%csl Gc3][%cal Rc2c4] } (3. c4 { The pawn gets hit and the c3 square is gone for your knight. } 3... b5 4. Nf3 b4 { His pawns come at you and your knight has no home. }) (3. Nf3 { Playable. But the queen knight is the one that wants to move first. } 3... b5 4. c4 Bb7 { Fine, and now the c pawn had to move anyway. }) (3. Bf4 { This is the one place the bishop must wait. He hits you first. } 3... d6 4. Nc3 e5 { The wall goes up and your bishop is short of squares. }) (3. e3 { Solid, and it turns down the big centre you were offered. } 3... e6 4. Nc3 exd5 { Level. Take the space while it is there. }) 3... g6 { He fianchettoes. Now take the whole middle. } 4. e4 { The full centre, and you are a long way ahead already. [%csl Ge4,Gd5] } (4. Nf3 { Developing first. Just as good, and the same plan. } 4... d6 5. e4 Bg7 { The pawn arrives anyway. Either order is fine. }) (4. a4 { Grabbing queen side space. Also playable. } 4... d6 5. Nf3 Bg7 { Equal value, but the middle is the bigger prize. }) (4. Bg5 { The pin, but his knight is not stuck to anything yet. } 4... d6 5. e4 Bg7 { You played e4 anyway and the bishop may get chased. }) (4. Bf4 { The London bishop, and it stares straight at his pawn wall. } 4... Bg7 5. a4 Nh5 { He kicks it and you lose time. The pawn was the move. }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he plays the Dutch"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/YY3yrlXn"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_-_0_1"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ Against the Dutch, refuse him a normal game. A pawn, for a real fight. }
1... f5 { The Dutch. He has a whole book ready. Give him something else to think about. } 2. e4 { The Staunton gambit. One pawn to drag him out of his preparation. [%cal Ge4f5] } (2. Bf4 { You can play the pyramid here too. It is fine, and quite popular. } 2... e6 3. e3 Nf6 { A good game, and one he knows well. The gambit is more fun. }) (2. c4 { A normal game, which is exactly what he was hoping for. } 2... Nf6 3. g3 e6 { Solid and better by the numbers. But he is in his book. }) (2. g3 { The main line against the Dutch. Also good and also well known. } 2... e6 3. Bg2 Nf6 { A long theoretical fight. The gambit avoids all of it. }) (2. Bg5 { A sharp try, and a well-respected one. } 2... h6 3. Bh4 g5 { Strong too. But the pawn offer is our weapon. }) (2. Nc3 { Also strong, and probably the most testing of all. } 2... Nf6 3. Bg5 d5 { A pleasant edge. But he is still inside his own book. }) (2. Nf3 { Quiet development. Perfectly good and completely normal. } 2... e6 3. g3 Be7 { Better by the numbers, and exactly the game he wanted. }) 2... fxe4 { He takes. Do not rush to win it back. } 3. Nc3 { Bring pieces out and let him do the worrying. [%csl Gc3] } (3. f3 { Taking at once. Playable, but the pin is stronger. } 3... exf3 4. Nxf3 Nf6 { Level. The knight first keeps far more pressure on. }) (3. Bg5 { The bishop before the knight. Also playable. } 3... d5 4. c4 Nf6 { Equal. The knight first covers the pawn's escape. }) (3. Bf4 { The London bishop, and here it is on the wrong square. } 3... d5 4. f3 Nf6 { He builds a wall and keeps the pawn. }) (3. c4 { Grabbing space with a pawn already missing. } 3... e6 4. Nc3 Nf6 { He is a pawn up and solid. You are worse. }) 3... Nf6 { The only good move he has. Now pin it. } 4. Bg5 { Pin the piece that guards the pawn. It is not running anywhere. [%cal Gg5f6] } (4. f3 { Grabbing straight away. Playable, but the pin is better. } 4... d5 5. fxe4 dxe4 { Level. He holds the pawn on e4 comfortably. }) (4. Be3 { The bishop does nothing there. It belongs on g5. } 4... e6 5. a3 d5 { He builds a wall and keeps the extra pawn. }) (4. Bf4 { The wrong diagonal: the knight is the piece to pin. } 4... e6 5. f3 Bb4 { He develops with a pin of his own. You are worse. }) (4. a3 { A quiet move with a pawn missing. Far too slow. } 4... d5 5. f3 Nc6 { He consolidates and you have nothing for the pawn. }) 4... g6 { He makes a home for his bishop. Finish developing. } 5. Bc4 { Out it comes, pointing at f7. It is roughly balanced, and it is a real fight. [%cal Gc4f7] } (5. h4 { Sharp, and about equal too. But get a piece out first. } 5... d5 6. h5 Bf5 { Also playable. Development is the safer road. }) (5. d5 { Space instead of pressure. Also equal and perfectly good. } 5... d6 6. Nge2 Bg7 { Fine. The bishop on c4 asks a sharper question. }) (5. f3 { Fine, but a shade worse than developing. The pawn is not running. } 5... d5 6. Qd2 Bg7 { Level. Get the pieces out first. }) (5. Bxf6 { Trading off your good bishop for no reason. } 5... exf6 6. Nxe4 d5 { He wins the pawn back and your pieces are gone. }) 1-0
